      "properties": {
        "Node name for S&R": "Note"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "Expand an image (outpainting)\n\nExtends the background of an existing image to a new aspect ratio, for example a portrait image into a 16:9 wallpaper. The original pixels are kept exactly; only the new area is generated.\n\n1. Drag the PNG onto the Image to expand node.\n2. In Pad image, set how many pixels to add on each side (multiples of 8). For a 1248x1824 image from the hires workflows:\n   - 16:9 landscape: left 1000, right 1000\n   - 16:10 landscape: left 840, right 840\n   - Square: left 288, right 288\n   - 9:16 phone: top 248, bottom 248\n   For other sizes: new width = height x 16 / 9, then split the difference between left and right.\n3. In the Background prompt, describe only the scenery that should continue (flower field, sky, petals), plus the artist tags of the original. Leave out character tags, or the model draws extra people.\n4. Press Run. The result is saved as expanded_xxxxx.png. The seed is random; run again for a different background.\n\nHow it works: the padded canvas is scaled down to about 1.3 megapixels, the new area is generated there, then scaled back up and blended under the original. The new area is therefore softer than the original, which suits backgrounds and depth of field. Large expansions (more than the original's width on each side) work for scenery but get less coherent; expand in two steps if needed.\n\nfeathering 200 blends the new area into the outer band of the original so no seam shows. If a seam still shows, raise it to 300; for small expansions (under 300 px a side) lower it to about 100."
      ],
      "title": "How to use"
    }
